\begin_body

\begin_layout Title
Statistical significance test between 2 groups of binary data
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
There are two approaches to reach this equation,
 from the mean of repeated Bernoulli trials (each trial is one observation x) or from a Gaussian approximation of a Binomial distribution (the number of successes is our only observation).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Mean of repeated Bernoulli trials
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The 𝑧-score of the mean is
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
𝑧 & = & \frac{𝑥¯−𝜇}{\sqrt{𝜎^{2}/𝑛}}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The Bernoulli distribution has 
\begin_inset Formula $𝜇=𝑝$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $𝜎^{2}=𝑝(1−𝑝)$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The mean,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{𝑥}=\frac{1}{n}∑_{i}𝑛=1𝑥_{𝑖}$
\end_inset

 is just the estimated probability 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{p}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
With 
\begin_inset Formula $𝑝=𝑝_{0}$
\end_inset

:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
𝑧 & = & \frac{\hat{p}−𝑝_{0}}{\sqrt{\frac{p_{0}(1−p_{0})}{n}}}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As 𝑛 is large,
 use the Central Limit Theorem stating that as 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{𝑥}$
\end_inset

 approaches a Gaussian distribution the corresponding 𝑝-value for the 𝑧-score can be found in a table for the Gaussian distribution.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Gaussian approximation of Binomial distribution
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The 𝑧-score for an observation is defined as
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
𝑧 & = & \frac{𝑥−𝜇_{𝑋}}{𝜎_{𝑋}}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where 
\begin_inset Formula $𝜇_{𝑋}$
\end_inset

 is the expected value and 
\begin_inset Formula $𝜎_{𝑋}$
\end_inset

 is the standard deviation of the random variable 𝑋.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In this case,
 𝑥 is the sum of the outcomes of the 𝑛 trials or,
 to be more specific,
 the count of "event"s .
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This is how the binomial distribution is defined with 
\begin_inset Formula $𝜇_{𝑋}=𝑛𝑝$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $𝜎_{𝑋}=\sqrt{𝑛𝑝(1−𝑝_{0})}$
\end_inset

.
 Substituting that into the definition of the 𝑧-score with 
\begin_inset Formula $𝑝=𝑝_{0}$
\end_inset

 gives:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
𝑧 & = & \frac{𝑥−𝑛𝑝_{0}}{\sqrt{𝑛𝑝_{0}(1−𝑝_{0})}}\\
 & = & \frac{𝑥/𝑛−𝑝_{0}}{𝑝_{0}(1−𝑝_{0})𝑛}\\
 & = & \frac{\hat{p}−𝑝_{0}}{𝑝_{0}(1−𝑝_{0})𝑛}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{p}$
\end_inset

 is just the estimated probability;
 the count of "event"s divided by the number of trials.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For large 𝑛,
 the binomial distribution can be approximated by a Gaussian and the 𝑧-score can be directly looked up in a 𝑝-value table for the Gaussian distribution.
\end_layout

